/// \defgroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b Behavior modules and their factory functions (O to S)
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Fourteen of BFME 2's behavior module classes, from
///        OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior to SpawnUnitBehavior, the instance
///        factories ten of them register with the module factory, and the
///        INI data factories of twelve of their module data classes.
///
/// A behavior module gives an object one piece of game behavior. Object
/// INI lists it on a module line
/// (`Behavior = RebuildHoleBehavior ModuleTag_...`); the tag after the name is the
/// author's choice and identifies the module within its object
/// (\ref page_modding_modules). This group documents the classes at the
/// level of their role and INI contract, and the code that creates the
/// modules and their data. The modules' own update, damage and death logic
/// is separate reverse-engineering work and is described here only where a
/// class page says so. BFME 2 registers many other behavior modules; only
/// the classes listed below are covered.
///
/// \par The factory flow
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance
/// factory, a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the
/// registrations are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`.
/// The data factory runs while INI loads (ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI),
/// the instance factory when an object is built (ModuleFactory::newModule).
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes this flow step
/// by step and \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories the shared code
/// shape of both factories; both apply here. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// - Every name in this group is spelled exactly like its class and is
///   registered as a logic (behavior) module.
/// - Read with Zero Hour's bit values (`Common/Module.h`), the interface
///   masks are: update only for OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior,
///   PassiveAreaEffectBehavior, PhysicsBehavior, RampageBehavior,
///   ShareExperienceBehavior, SiegeDockingBehavior, SlaveWatcherBehavior
///   and SpawnUnitBehavior; update and die for SlowDeathBehavior,
///   ShipSlowDeathBehavior and RebuildHoleBehavior; update and damage for
///   PoisonedBehavior; update and upgrade for ReplenishUnitsBehavior.
///   RunOffMapBehavior registers an empty mask. The BFME 2 meaning of the
///   bits is not confirmed here, but the three classes that also exist in
///   Zero Hour with the same base interfaces (PoisonedBehavior,
///   RebuildHoleBehavior and SlowDeathBehavior) fit the Zero Hour reading,
///   and so does ReplenishUnitsBehavior, whose INI block takes the shared
///   upgrade fields.
/// - Some other factories registered under these names (for example
///   PhysicsBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///   SlowDeathBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance and
///   SpawnUnitBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData) are not documented
///   in this group.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory here has the same frame: allocate the class's
///   size with the global \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory), run the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data inside an
///   exception frame that frees the block if the constructor throws, and
///   return the result as a Module pointer. When the allocation returns
///   null the constructor is skipped and null is returned. None of the ten
///   adjusts the returned pointer.
/// - Eleven of the twelve data factories here share one frame as well:
///   allocate, run the data class's default constructor inside an
///   exception frame, then, when the INI pointer is not null, call
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc with the new data and the class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse. Parsing happens after the exception frame has
///   closed, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new
///   data. The parse step does not test the allocation: a null block with a
///   non-null INI pointer would still be handed to the parser. Zero Hour's
///   data factory macro has the same order.
/// - OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData is the
///   short form, with no exception frame: its constructor is inlined into
///   the factory (see that function).
/// - SiegeDockingBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData calls a
///   store-only constructor that the data factories of two other module
///   data classes call as well (see that function).
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template.
///   It is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from the
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Origins
/// PhysicsBehavior, PoisonedBehavior, RebuildHoleBehavior and
/// SlowDeathBehavior exist in Zero Hour; PhysicsBehavior has kept the name
/// but not the INI fields (see its page). The other ten are not in Zero
/// Hour. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has code for all of them except
/// ShipSlowDeathBehavior, so those classes already existed in BFME 1.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags and each
///   class's fields. The field lists on the class pages are read from the
///   retail field tables, so they are complete for the tables they name.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names and what each class does with its
///   fields. A new kind of behavior needs a new class and a new
///   ModuleFactory::init registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Multiplayer: these are logic modules and their data comes from
///   \c Object blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Many fields here are converted to logic-frame units once, when the INI
/// is read: durations parsed by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt or
/// INI::parseDurationReal (for example \c PingDelay, \c ReplenishDelay,
/// \c PoisonDuration, \c FadeOutTime, \c SinkDelay and
/// \c WorkerRespawnDelay) and speeds parsed by INI::parseVelocityReal
/// (\c SinkRate). Fields read as plain integers are not converted by the
/// parser. Among them, RampageBehavior counts \c RampageLifeTimer,
/// \c RampageAngryLifeTimer and \c RampageResetTimer down once per
/// update, and that update asks to run again on the next logic frame, so
/// they are frame counts (see RampageBehavior). A change to the logic rate
/// (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore affects the two kinds of field
/// differently.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior": an
///        update module with a single fade-out time.
///
/// Its data class reads one field, \c FadeOutTime, a duration written in
/// milliseconds and stored as logic frames; the constructor's default is
/// one frame. OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior::update does its work only while
/// its object has a certain KindOf flag, not identified here. It then asks
/// to run again on the next logic frame while the object has that flag and
/// once per logic second otherwise; once the object is effectively dead it
/// returns the sleep-forever value instead, so it does not run again unless
/// something wakes the module. On the first update that does its work,
/// unless the object has a certain status (not identified here) or has no
/// AI, it gives the object an AI command, not described here, and makes it
/// unselectable; the module records the attempt either way, so this step
/// is not retried on later updates. On each such update a fraction that
/// starts at 1 drops by one \c FadeOutTime-th (a \c FadeOutTime of zero
/// counts as one frame), so it runs out after about \c FadeOutTime frames.
/// While the fraction lasts it is written to the object's drawable; going
/// by the class name it sets how far the object has faded, which this page
/// does not confirm. When it runs out, the module may notify another object
/// whose ID its object holds (under conditions not described here) and
/// then destroys its object.
/// BFME 1 already has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior: the fade-out time.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared
/// by every object of the template. It adds no virtual functions of its
/// own: its virtual table is the one several other module data classes
/// share.

/// \fn Module *OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *OathbreakersFadeAwayBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior":
///        creates the module data and parses the module's INI block into
///        it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. Unlike the other data factories
/// in this group it has no exception frame: the data class's constructor
/// is inlined into it and only stores the shared virtual table, the
/// \c FadeOutTime default of one logic frame and nothing that could throw.
/// When \p ini is not null, the block is parsed with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the class's \c buildFieldParse, whose one
/// table holds \c FadeOutTime. The allocation result only decides whether
/// the constructor's stores run; as in the other factories, it is not
/// checked before the parse step.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor default.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see OathbreakersFadeAwayBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class PassiveAreaEffectBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "PassiveAreaEffectBehavior": an
///        update module that, at a fixed interval, refreshes a list of
///        affected objects and applies its effect to them; configured by a
///        radius, an object filter and healing, modifier and FX fields.
///
/// The BFME 2 update shows the cycle. The module wakes every
/// \c PingDelay; a \c PingDelay of zero is treated as one frame. When
/// \c UpgradeRequired is set, it does nothing until its object has that
/// upgrade, and it also stands down while its object is in certain states.
/// Once per \c PingDelay it refreshes the list of objects it affects:
/// objects within \c EffectRadius of its object that pass \c AllowFilter,
/// together with fixed checks not described here; its object itself is
/// never on the list. On every wake, for each object on that list that
/// still exists and is not effectively dead, it runs two pulses:
/// - A healing pulse, when \c HealPercentPerSecond is above zero, two
///   body values that the reconstruction reads as current and maximum
///   health differ, and the object passes a check not described here. The
///   amount is the second of those values times \c HealPercentPerSecond
///   divided by the logic frame rate, multiplied by \c PingDelay when that
///   is not zero; on that reading, \c HealPercentPerSecond is a share of
///   maximum health per second. It is applied through
///   Object::attemptHealingFromSoleBenefactor with the module's own object
///   as the healer, and \c HealFX then plays on the object. When
///   \c NonStackable is set, the pulse is also skipped while a frame
///   number kept by the object's body, plus \c PingDelay, is still ahead
///   of the current frame; which event that frame number records is not
///   confirmed here.
/// - An attribute pulse: each \c ModifierName entry is added to the
///   object's attribute-modifier pool (Object::addAttributeModifierToPool).
///   Then, if the object has an AttributeModifierPoolUpdate module, the
///   \c AntiCategories flags are passed to it with an expiry of the current
///   frame plus \c PingDelay, and \c AntiFX plays on the object. What the
///   pool does with those flags is not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c EffectRadius (real), \c PingDelay (duration),
/// \c HealPercentPerSecond (percentage), \c ModifierName (a list of names;
/// each line adds to it), \c AllowFilter (an object filter),
/// \c UpgradeRequired (an upgrade name stored as text), \c NonStackable
/// (boolean), \c AntiCategories (a set of category flags, read by its own
/// bit-flag list parser, not an FX list; the accepted flag names are not
/// listed here), \c AntiFX and \c HealFX (FX lists). BFME 1 already has
/// the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see PassiveAreaEffectBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      PassiveAreaEffectBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class PassiveAreaEffectBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for PassiveAreaEffectBehavior: radius, interval,
///        filter, upgrade and effect fields.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// PassiveAreaEffectBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *PassiveAreaEffectBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "PassiveAreaEffectBehavior":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's PassiveAreaEffectBehaviorModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see PassiveAreaEffectBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *PassiveAreaEffectBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "PassiveAreaEffectBehavior": creates
///        the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame; the class's \c buildFieldParse adds one table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see PassiveAreaEffectBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class PhysicsBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "PhysicsBehavior": in BFME 2 its
///        INI block holds flight-curve, bounce, tumble and shock-stun
///        fields rather than Zero Hour's mass and friction fields.
///
/// Zero Hour's PhysicsBehavior (`PhysicsUpdate.cpp`) is the general
/// physics of an object, configured by \c Mass, the friction fields,
/// \c ShockResistance and others. BFME 2 registers a module under the same
/// name, but the data factory it registers parses a different table, and
/// none of \c Mass, \c ForwardFriction, \c ShockResistance or
/// \c AerodynamicFriction occurs as a string in the retail executable.
/// The fields that remain resemble those of Zero Hour's
/// DumbProjectileBehavior (a two-point flight curve given by heights and
/// percent indents) plus bounce and ground-hit settings. How much of Zero
/// Hour's physics code BFME 2 keeps under this class is not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c TumbleRandomly, \c AllowBouncing,
/// \c KillWhenRestingOnGround, \c OrientToFlightPath and
/// \c IgnoreTerrainHeight (booleans); \c GravityMult, \c FirstHeight,
/// \c SecondHeight, \c BounceFirstHeight, \c BounceSecondHeight and
/// \c CurveFlattenMinDist (reals); \c FirstPercentIndent,
/// \c SecondPercentIndent, \c BounceFirstPercentIndent,
/// \c BounceSecondPercentIndent, \c FirstPercentHeight and
/// \c SecondPercentHeight (percentages); \c ShockStunnedTimeLow,
/// \c ShockStunnedTimeHigh and \c ShockStandingTime (durations);
/// \c BounceCount (integer); \c GroundHitFX and \c GroundBounceFX (FX
/// lists). Zero Hour mods that set \c Mass or friction on this module do
/// not carry over.
///
/// \see PhysicsBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class PhysicsBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for PhysicsBehavior: flight-curve, bounce and
///        shock-stun fields.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// PhysicsBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *PhysicsBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "PhysicsBehavior": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame; the class's \c buildFieldParse adds one table, the
/// fields listed on the PhysicsBehavior page.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see PhysicsBehavior


/// \class PoisonedBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "PoisonedBehavior": in Zero Hour,
///        keeps damaging its object for a while after the object takes
///        poison damage; BFME 2 keeps its member names and INI fields.
///
/// Zero Hour: PoisonedBehavior is an update module that is also a damage
/// module. When its object takes poison damage it starts or refreshes the
/// poisoned state, then deals the damage actually dealt again, as
/// unresistable damage, every \c PoisonDamageInterval until
/// \c PoisonDuration has passed; healing ends the poisoning. BFME 2 keeps
/// these members under the same names (PoisonedBehavior::onDamage,
/// PoisonedBehavior::onHealing, PoisonedBehavior::startPoisonedEffects,
/// PoisonedBehavior::update), and its INI fields are the same two; this
/// page does not compare the bodies.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c PoisonDamageInterval and \c PoisonDuration, durations
/// written in milliseconds and stored as logic frames.
///
/// \see PoisonedBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class PoisonedBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for PoisonedBehavior: damage interval and duration.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// PoisonedBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *PoisonedBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "PoisonedBehavior": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame; the class's \c buildFieldParse adds one table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see PoisonedBehavior


/// \class RampageBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "RampageBehavior": an update module
///        that, when its object's health is low and falling, starts either
///        a rampage or an "angry" state, chosen by a nearby-enemy check;
///        optionally gated by upgrades.
///
/// RampageBehavior::update does nothing until the object has every
/// upgrade listed in \c RequiredUpgrade. A new episode can start only
/// when all of these hold: the ratio of current to maximum health (two
/// body values that the reconstruction reads as such) is below
/// \c RampageHealthThreshold, health is below the value the module last
/// recorded (that value starts very high and is refreshed to the current
/// health when the reset timer runs out), the reset timer has run out,
/// and the object is not already
/// rampaging or in certain other states. Low health is always required;
/// the nearby-enemy check never starts an episode on its own. It only
/// chooses the mode:
/// - When \c RampageEnemyThreshold is 0, or a count returned by an AI
///   query over \c RampageEnemyCheckRange (of enemies, going by the field
///   names) reaches \c RampageEnemyThreshold, the
///   object switches weapon set and is ordered to attack its own position,
///   for \c RampageLifeTimer updates.
/// - Otherwise it sets a model condition and gives the object an AI
///   command, neither identified here (the "angry" state of the field
///   name), for \c RampageAngryLifeTimer updates.
///
/// When that timer runs out, or certain object states cut it short, the
/// module ends the episode and starts \c RampageResetTimer; each timer
/// counts down by one per update. BFME 1 already has the class; Zero Hour
/// does not.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c RampageHealthThreshold and \c RampageEnemyCheckRange
/// (reals); \c RampageLifeTimer, \c RampageAngryLifeTimer and
/// \c RampageResetTimer (timers read as plain integers, not converted from
/// milliseconds by the parser); \c RampageEnemyThreshold (a plain integer);
/// \c RequiredUpgrade (a list of names).
///
/// \see RampageBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RampageBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RampageBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for RampageBehavior: thresholds, timers and the
///        required upgrades.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// RampageBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn Module *RampageBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RampageBehavior": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's RampageBehaviorModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see RampageBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *RampageBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RampageBehavior": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame; the class's \c buildFieldParse adds one table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see RampageBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class RebuildHoleBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "RebuildHoleBehavior": in Zero
///        Hour, the behavior of the hole a destroyed structure leaves, which
///        spawns a worker that rebuilds the structure; BFME 2 keeps its
///        member names and INI fields.
///
/// Zero Hour: when a structure with a rebuild-hole die module is
/// destroyed, a hole object takes its place; the hole's RebuildHoleBehavior
/// remembers the structure's template, spawns a worker of
/// \c WorkerObjectName, respawns it after \c WorkerRespawnDelay if it is
/// killed, and regenerates its own health at
/// \c HoleHealthRegen\%PerSecond. BFME 2 keeps members under the Zero Hour
/// names (RebuildHoleBehavior::startRebuildProcess,
/// RebuildHoleBehavior::newWorkerRespawnProcess,
/// RebuildHoleBehavior::getRebuildHoleBehaviorInterfaceFromObject), and
/// its INI fields are the same three; this page does not compare the
/// bodies.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c WorkerObjectName (an object name stored as text),
/// \c WorkerRespawnDelay (a duration stored as a real number of logic
/// frames) and \c HoleHealthRegen\%PerSecond (a percentage; the percent sign
/// is part of the field name).
///
/// \see RebuildHoleBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RebuildHoleBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for RebuildHoleBehavior: worker, respawn delay and
///        health regeneration.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// RebuildHoleBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *RebuildHoleBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RebuildHoleBehavior": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame; the class's \c buildFieldParse adds one table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see RebuildHoleBehavior


/// \class ReplenishUnitsBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "ReplenishUnitsBehavior": an
///        upgrade-gated update module that, at an interval, picks the
///        closest qualifying object of its own player nearby and plays an
///        FX on it, unless an enemy is close.
///
/// Its INI block takes the shared upgrade fields (\c TriggeredBy,
/// \c ConflictsWith, \c RequiresAllTriggers,
/// \c RequiresAllConflictingTriggers, \c CustomAnimAndDuration and
/// \c Permanent; \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories describes them)
/// as well as its own, and its registered mask includes the bit Zero Hour
/// uses for the upgrade interface.
///
/// ReplenishUnitsBehavior::update does nothing, and sleeps until woken,
/// unless the upgrade part reports the module active and the object is
/// alive; while active it wakes every \c ReplenishDelay. On each wake, in
/// this order:
/// - When \c ReplenishHordeMembersOnly is set, its object must supply
///   something to compare against (its horde, going by the field name);
///   when it supplies nothing, the module sleeps until woken, before any
///   enemy check.
/// - When \c NoReplenishIfEnemyWithinRadius is not zero and an object
///   that passes a relationship filter (enemies, going by the field name)
///   and further checks lies within that radius, nothing else happens on
///   this wake.
/// - Otherwise it picks the one closest object within \c ReplenishRadius
///   that belongs to its object's controlling player, passes a status
///   test built from \c ReplenishStatii (which also excludes one fixed
///   status), is alive, is not the module's own object and passes one
///   last filter built from the value supplied in the first step (none
///   when \c ReplenishHordeMembersOnly is not set).
/// - The chosen object gets \c ReplenishFXList played on it. When
///   \c ReplenishStatii includes one particular status flag, its
///   DetachableRiderUpdate module, if it has one, is also called; what
///   that call restores is not described here.
///
/// BFME 1 already has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c StartsActive and \c ReplenishHordeMembersOnly (booleans),
/// \c ReplenishDelay (a duration), \c ReplenishRadius and
/// \c NoReplenishIfEnemyWithinRadius (reals), \c ReplenishFXList (an FX
/// list) and \c ReplenishStatii, read by the same routine as the shared
/// die conditions' \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus (Zero Hour reads
/// those as object status masks).
///
/// \see ReplenishUnitsBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ReplenishUnitsBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ReplenishUnitsBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
