/// \class SlaveWatcherBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "SlaveWatcherBehavior": an update
///        module on a master object that watches one slave, can pass the
///        master's upgrades on to it, and changes the master's own
///        upgrades once the slave is gone.
///
/// The module sits on the watching (master) object and holds the object
/// ID of one slave. The ID is set by another function of the module, which
/// also copies the master's upgrades to the slave at once when
/// \c ShareUpgrades is set; this page does not cover what calls it. SlaveWatcherBehavior::update looks the
/// slave up while an ID is held:
/// - When the slave no longer exists or is effectively dead, the module
///   changes this object's own upgrades: it removes the upgrade named by
///   \c RemoveUpgrade and, going by the field name, grants the one named
///   by \c GrantUpgrade. It then refreshes this object's upgrade modules,
///   forgets the slave and returns the sleep-forever value, so it does not
///   run again unless something wakes the module.
/// - Otherwise, when \c ShareUpgrades is set and this object's upgrade set
///   has changed since the module last copied it, the module records the
///   new set, adds it to the slave's upgrades (a bitwise OR) and refreshes
///   the slave's upgrade modules.
///
/// When the module is destroyed (~SlaveWatcherBehavior) and still holds a
/// slave ID, it kills that slave if it still exists, unless
/// \c LetSlaveLive is set. BFME 1 already has the class; Zero Hour does
/// not.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c GrantUpgrade and \c RemoveUpgrade (upgrade names stored
/// as text) and \c ShareUpgrades and \c LetSlaveLive (booleans). Because
/// \c ShareUpgrades merges with a bitwise OR, it only adds upgrades to the
/// slave; this module never takes one away from the slave, even after the
/// master loses it.
///
/// \see SlaveWatcherBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      SlaveWatcherBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class SlaveWatcherBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for SlaveWatcherBehavior: upgrade names and two
///        flags.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// SlaveWatcherBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *SlaveWatcherBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SlaveWatcherBehavior": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame; this factory differs only in
/// allocating a larger module.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's SlaveWatcherBehaviorModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SlaveWatcherBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *SlaveWatcherBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "SlaveWatcherBehavior": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame; the class's \c buildFieldParse adds one table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see SlaveWatcherBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class SlowDeathBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "SlowDeathBehavior": configured by
///        sink, fling, delay, FX, OCL and weapon fields; in Zero Hour, a
///        death sequence that keeps a dying object in the world for a while
///        before it is destroyed. Its data class is the base of BFME 2's
///        other slow-death module data classes.
///
/// Zero Hour: SlowDeathBehavior is an update module that is also a die
/// module. When its object dies, the slow-death modules whose die
/// conditions apply compete by weighted random choice
/// (\c ProbabilityModifier, raised by overkill through
/// \c ModifierBonusPerOverkillPercent), so the choice uses the logic random
/// stream (one roll from one to the sum of the modifiers); the winner then
/// runs its phases on timers.
///
/// BFME 2: SlowDeathBehavior::onDie also chooses by weighted random
/// choice on the logic random stream. It collects every behavior module of
/// the object whose slow-death interface passes
/// SlowDeathBehavior::isDieApplicable, weighting each by
/// SlowDeathBehavior::getProbabilityModifier (\c ProbabilityModifier plus
/// the overkill bonus from \c ModifierBonusPerOverkillPercent, never less
/// than 1), and draws one by weight. Zero Hour rolls once and starts
/// whichever module the roll lands on, re-testing isDieApplicable as it
/// walks the modules. BFME 2 instead tests the drawn module with
/// SlowDeathBehavior::isDieApplicable and one further check, not
/// identified here, before starting its slow death
/// (SlowDeathBehavior::beginSlowDeath). A module that fails is dropped and
/// the draw repeats among the rest, so a death can take more than one
/// random draw, and when every candidate fails none starts.
/// SlowDeathBehavior::beginSlowDeath and
/// SlowDeathBehavior::update, which run the chosen death, are not
/// described here.
///
/// The BFME 2 slow-death subclasses include ShipSlowDeathBehavior,
/// GiantBirdSlowDeathBehavior and ClearanceTestingSlowDeathBehavior: their
/// data classes first add everything SlowDeathBehaviorModuleData accepts,
/// so their INI blocks take these fields too.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Own fields: \c SinkRate (a speed, converted per frame),
///   \c ProbabilityModifier (integer), \c ModifierBonusPerOverkillPercent
///   (percentage); \c SinkDelay, \c SinkDelayVariance,
///   \c DestructionDelay, \c DestructionDelayVariance, \c DecayBeginTime,
///   \c FadeDelay and \c FadeTime (durations); \c FX, \c OCL, \c Weapon and
///   \c Sound (each read by its own parser); \c FlingForce and
///   \c FlingForceVariance (reals); \c FlingPitch and
///   \c FlingPitchVariance (angles); \c DeathFlags (its own parser);
///   \c ShadowWhenDead (boolean).
/// - Shared die conditions, added by the same \c buildFieldParse:
///   \c DeathTypes, \c ExemptStatus, \c RequiredStatus,
///   \c DamageAmountRequired, \c MinKillerAngle and \c MaxKillerAngle.
///   Zero Hour's shared die table has \c DeathTypes, \c VeterancyLevels,
///   \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus, so \c VeterancyLevels is not
///   among the BFME 2 die conditions here, and \c DamageAmountRequired,
///   \c MinKillerAngle and \c MaxKillerAngle are new.
/// - Compared with Zero Hour's own table, \c DestructionAltitude is gone
///   and \c DecayBeginTime, \c Sound, \c DeathFlags, \c ShadowWhenDead,
///   \c FadeDelay and \c FadeTime are new.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// BFME 2 picks among slow-death modules with the logic random stream
/// (\ref page_core_frameworks_random). A change to the set, order or
/// weights of slow-death modules on an object changes which module a draw
/// picks, and can change how many draws a death takes, and so breaks
/// replays and lockstep with unchanged clients.
///
/// \see SlowDeathBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///      ShipSlowDeathBehavior

/// \class SlowDeathBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for SlowDeathBehavior and the base of the other
///        slow-death module data classes.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// SlowDeathBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData, or by a subclass's
/// own data factory, and shared by every object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *SlowDeathBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "SlowDeathBehavior": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame. The class's \c buildFieldParse adds two tables: its own
/// slow-death fields, then the shared die-condition table at the offset of
/// the data's die-condition part (both listed on the SlowDeathBehavior
/// page). Subclass data factories do not call this function; they build
/// their own data and reach these tables through their own
/// \c buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see SlowDeathBehavior


/// \class SpawnUnitBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_b
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "SpawnUnitBehavior": an update
///        module configured by a unit name, a command and a spawn-once
///        flag.
///
/// The field names suggest that the module creates objects of
/// \c UnitName, gives them \c UnitCommand, and, with \c SpawnOnce, does so
/// only once; this page does not confirm the update logic, and the data
/// factory is not documented in this group. BFME 1 already has the class;
/// Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c UnitName and \c UnitCommand (stored as text) and
/// \c SpawnOnce (boolean).
///
/// \see SpawnUnitBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *SpawnUnitBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SpawnUnitBehavior": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SpawnUnitBehavior
